 "context": "<figure id=\"fig-ch39_03_01\" class=\" \"><span data-type=\"media\" id=\"fs-idp140413136\" data-alt=\" Illustration A shows the direction of airflow in both inhalation and exhalation in birds. During inhalation, air passes from the beak down the trachea to the posterior air sac located behind the lungs. From the posterior air sac, air enters the lungs, and the anterior air sac in front of the lungs. Air from both air sacs also enters hollows in bones. During exhalation air from hollows in the bones enters the air sacs, then the lungs, then the trachea, where it exits through the beaks. Illustration B compares a dinosaur and a bird. Both have anterior air sacs in front of the lungs, and posterior air sacs behind them. The air sacs connect to hollow openings in bones.\">\n<img src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20241024.164013/resources/1ba4210bae087cebfb533bc4e1d7011b7dcb8f4a\" data-media-type=\"image/jpg\" alt=\" Illustration A shows the direction of airflow in both inhalation and exhalation in birds. During inhalation, air passes from the beak down the trachea to the posterior air sac located behind the lungs. From the posterior air sac, air enters the lungs, and the anterior air sac in front of the lungs. Air from both air sacs also enters hollows in bones. During exhalation air from hollows in the bones enters the air sacs, then the lungs, then the trachea, where it exits through the beaks. Illustration B compares a dinosaur and a bird. Both have anterior air sacs in front of the lungs, and posterior air sacs behind them. The air sacs connect to hollow openings in bones.\" width=\"480\" height=\"533\">\n</span>\n</figure>",
 "stimulus_html": "",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 529363,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Recall and review the exhalation pathway of birds, as illustrated. Determine what is meant by the statement, “these animals have a ‘flow-through respiratory system,’” and explain how this aids birds in flight.",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1036116,
     "content_html": "The “flow-through respiratory system” allows air to move only entirely in one direction, without retracing its pathway at all, allowing the animal to breathe faster while in flight."
    },
    {
     "id": 1036117,
     "content_html": "The “flow-through respiratory system” contains several air sacs that make the animal lighter compared to similar-sized animals with no air sacs."
    },
    {
     "id": 1036118,
     "content_html": "The “flow-through respiratory system” contains many lungs, which allow the animals to take in the large quantities of oxygen needed for flight."
    },
    {
     "id": 1036119,
     "content_html": "The “flow-through respiratory system” allows air to move in an almost unidirectional pathway to reach a series of air sacs that provide air to hollow bones, making the animals lighter and providing efficient respiration."
    }
   ],
